#
# Conformance case: transaction/effect-applies-as-supersession.
#
# An action schema declares a logic:effect that ins(balanceHigh) and del(balanceLow).
# A logic:TransactionStep instantiates the schema from state0 to state1. The successor
# state1 asserts the carried-forward support (accountOpen), the inserted support
# (balanceHigh), and NOT the deleted one (balanceLow). The retired balanceLow is
# recorded via the append-only supersession quartet (logic:activeInState,
# logic:validUntilState, logic:retiredByTransaction, logic:supersededBy) and the
# predecessor state0 STILL carries it — del is supersession, never erasure.
#
# CRITICAL: the teleology evaluator seeds ONLY from input.nq world facts; this
# input.logic.ttl carries the minimal, clean-compiling, certifiable program axiom the
# projections need (no asserted teleology triples here — the world facts live in
# input.nq, exactly as the foundation conformance cases keep theirs).

@prefix logic: <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/logic/> .
@prefix ex:    <https://example.org/transaction/effect-applies-as-supersession/> .

ex:GoalExpression a logic:GoalExpression .

# The compile-time reasoning contract selects the Evolution facet that classifies this
# transaction program. logic:TransactionPathEvolution drives certification to the
# transaction-path decidability class (conflict-serializability; PTIME classification,
# not search).
ex:TransactionContract a logic:ReasoningContract ;
    logic:evolution logic:TransactionPathEvolution .
